    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Christianity began among the followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus of Nazareth</a>, a Jewish teacher crucified in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> around 30 CE, whom they proclaimed risen from the dead, the Messiah and the Son of God.</li><li>Spread by apostles such as Paul across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, it survived persecution, became the empire’s religion under Constantine and Theodosius in the 4th century and shaped the civilisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</li><li>Divided between East and West in 1054 and again by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the 16th century, it spread worldwide with missions and empires and is today the largest religion, with about 2.3 billion followers.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The custom of counting years from the birth of Christ, “anno Domini”, was devised by the monk Dionysius Exiguus in 525; he probably placed the birth a few years too late, which is why historians date it to about 6–4 BCE.</p></aside>
    </section>

